// The same cloud as `soft_points`, drawn as streaks instead of sprites.
//
// This file is the first thing in the repository that draws something other
// than a point sprite. It pairs with `drift_shell.kir` — **the same L1, byte
// for byte, as `soft_points.kir` pairs with**. Nothing about the geometry
// changed; only the renderer did:
//
//   karakuri-cli examples/drift_shell.kir examples/soft_points.kir     sprites
//   karakuri-cli examples/drift_shell.kir examples/drift_streaks.kir   streaks
//
// The defaults below are set for `drift_shell`'s own default of 262144
// elements, where a quarter of a million overlapping strokes read as brushed
// fur. To see the primitive itself — separate strokes, each one a segment —
// thin the population out and lengthen them:
//
//   karakuri-cli examples/drift_shell.kir examples/drift_streaks.kir \
//     --capacity 16384 --param exposure=0.6 --param streak=1.2 --param width=0.00222
//
// **How an L4 says it draws lines.** It assigns `clip_b`, the far end of a
// segment, alongside `clip`. There is no `topology` declaration on an L4 and
// no second thing to keep in step: writing a second endpoint is the whole of
// the declaration. Assign it and every element is a segment; leave it out and
// every element is a sprite.
//
// **Where the far end comes from is this file's own business.** The IR has no
// notion of one element linking to another — a segment's two ends both belong
// to one element, which is what lets a streak survive spawning and killing
// material, where an index into the element buffer would not. Here the far end
// is the element's own position pushed back along its velocity, so each stroke
// is the path that element is on. Other useful far ends: a fixed direction (a
// rain field), a point on a curve one step behind (a strand — see
// `strand_shell.kir`), or the origin (a burst).
//
// **`point_rate` is the width of the stroke as a fraction of the frame's
// height**, the same number and the same unit a sprite's extent uses.
//
// **`streak` does not go to zero, and that is not a rounding of the range.** A
// segment whose two ends coincide is zero-area and draws nothing, where a
// sprite at zero velocity is still a sprite — so `streak = 0` would be a fader
// that blanks the slot rather than one that shortens the stroke. The floor is
// the shortest stroke that is still a stroke. (Elements whose `velocity` is
// zero disappear at any setting, for the same reason and with nothing to set.)
//
// **`point_coord` runs along the segment in x and across it in y**, both in
// [0, 1]. So the falloff below is `abs(point_coord.y * 2 - 1)` — distance from
// the centre *line*, not from a centre point — and `point_coord.x` is what
// tapers the stroke from head to tail.
//
// Exposure sits below `soft_points`' because a streak covers many more texels
// than the sprite it replaces, and this blend is additive.

proc drift_streaks {
  kind  L4
  blend additive

  consumes position, velocity

  param width    : float [0.00069, 0.0333] = 0.00167
  param streak   : float [0.05, 2.0] = 0.70
  param hue      : float [0.0, 1.0]  = 0.55
  param spread   : float [0.0, 1.0]  = 0.30
  param exposure : float [0.0, 8.0]  = 0.10
  param falloff  : float [0.5, 8.0]  = 2.0
  param taper    : float [0.0, 1.0]  = 0.85

  vertex {
    // The head is where the element is; the tail is where it was, near enough.
    // `velocity` here is the curl field `drift_shell` emits, so a stroke lies
    // along the flow rather than along the rotation the shell is under — the
    // rotation moves the whole cloud and would give every stroke the same
    // direction.
    let tail = position - velocity * streak;

    clip   = camera * vec4(position, 1.0);
    clip_b = camera * vec4(tail, 1.0);

    // Faster elements are drawn heavier, as in `soft_points` — the same
    // reading of `velocity`, applied to width instead of to extent.
    point_rate = width * (0.35 + 0.65 * clamp(length(velocity) * 0.6, 0.0, 1.0));
  }

  fragment {
    // Across the stroke: 0 at the centre line, 1 at either edge.
    let across = abs(point_coord.y * 2.0 - 1.0);
    let edge   = pow(max(0.0, 1.0 - across), falloff);

    // Along the stroke: 1 at the head, fading toward the tail. This is what
    // makes a streak read as motion rather than as a stick.
    let along = 1.0 - taper * point_coord.x;

    let c = hsv_to_rgb(vec3(hue + hash1(seed) * spread, 0.75, 1.0));
    color = vec4(c * exposure, edge * along);
  }
}
